---
/**
 * `/methodology/` — SPEC.md §6, §8.
 *
 * The public home of every formula the site computes with. The constants table
 * is generated from `allConstants()` rather than retyped here, so the published
 * methodology cannot drift away from the numbers the model actually uses — the
 * failure mode where a site documents one thing and computes another.
 */
import Section from '../components/blocks/Section.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../components/ui/BaseLayout.astro';
import { allConstants } from '../lib/math/dynamics/constants.ts';
import { RISK_DISCLAIMER, RISK_WEIGHTS } from '../lib/math/riskIndex.ts';
import { SCORE_METHODOLOGY_NOTE, SCORE_WEIGHTS } from '../lib/math/score.ts';
import { DECAY_BY_POSITIONING } from '../lib/math/depreciation.ts';

const constants = allConstants();

const formulae = [
  { name: 'Drag force', formula: 'F_d = ½ · ρ · Cd · A · v²' },
  { name: 'Rolling resistance', formula: 'F_r = Crr · m · g' },
  { name: 'Top speed', formula: 'solve P_wheel = ½ρ·Cd·A·v³ + Crr·m·g·v for v' },
  { name: 'Braking distance', formula: 'd = v² / (2 · μ · g)' },
  { name: 'Power-to-weight', formula: 'P / m' },
  { name: 'Drag coefficient from force', formula: 'Cd = 2F / (ρ · U² · A)' },
  { name: 'Frontal area (estimated)', formula: 'A ≈ 0.85 · width · height' },
  { name: 'Steady-state consumption', formula: '(F_d + F_r) · v ÷ (η_drivetrain · η_powertrain)' },
  { name: 'Depreciation', formula: 'value(t) = price · (1 − rate)^t · mileageFactor · conditionFactor' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="Every formula Markey computes with, published rather than hidden — including where the model is weakest."
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="type-title text-[clamp(1.75rem,5vw,2.5rem)]">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="type-body mt-3 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary">
      Every figure this site calculates is published as a formula, not asserted
      as a result. If you disagree with a number here, you can check the working.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section
    title="What “modelled” means"
    lede="The most important thing on this page."
  >
    <div class="type-body flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-3 py-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <p>
        Where a manufacturer published a figure, that is what the spec sheet
        shows. A modelled figure never overwrites a real one — it appears
        alongside it, labelled as the model's output, which incidentally makes
        the comparison interesting in its own right. A car that beats its own
        published 0–100, or badly misses it, is telling you something.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where an input the physics needs is missing, you get a gap rather than a
        guess. A car with no published drag coefficient anywhere gets no
        aerodynamic panel at all.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink">
        <strong>How accurate is it?</strong> Checked against published figures
        for cars across the range, the acceleration model lands within roughly
        20% in both directions, and the top-speed model within about 5% for
        cars that are not electronically limited. It is useful for comparing
        cars on a consistent basis. It is not a substitute for a road test.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Units">
    <div class="type-body flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-3 py-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <p>
        Everything is stored in SI. Imperial figures are computed for display
        when you ask for them and are never stored or indexed, so the two cannot
        drift apart. The schema only accepts SI units, which means a horsepower
        figure cannot physically be saved into the database by mistake.
      </p>
      <p>
        Power converts to <strong class="text-ink">metric horsepower (PS)</strong>
        by default rather than mechanical bhp, because the overwhelming majority
        of published figures for European and Japanese cars are PS. The two
        differ by about 1.4%, and quoting one as the other is a small error
        repeated endlessly elsewhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Formulae" lede="All standard, textbook automotive physics.">
    <dl class="flex flex-col gap-3 py-5">
      {
        formulae.map((item) => (
          <div class="grid gap-1 border-b border-line pb-3 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0 sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-4">
            <dt class="text-sm text-ink-secondary">{item.name}</dt>
            <dd class="font-mono text-sm break-words">{item.formula}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section
    title="Default constants"
    lede="Every one of these is a reasoned default, not a measurement. Each carries the reasoning that justifies it, and each is a candidate for revision as the catalog grows."
  >
    <dl class="flex flex-col gap-4 py-5">
      {
        constants.map((constant) => (
          <div class="border-b border-line pb-4 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <dt class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3">
              <span class="type-heading text-sm">{constant.name}</span>
              <span class="type-data text-sm tabular-nums text-ink-secondary">
                {constant.value}
                {constant.unit ? ` ${constant.unit}` : ''}
              </span>
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
              {constant.basis}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section
    title="Known limitations of the dynamics model"
    lede="Stated plainly, because a model whose weaknesses are hidden is worse than no model."
  >
    <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 py-5 pl-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">It does not know about gearing.</strong> Top
        speed is where power balances drag. A car geared out below that point
        will never reach it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">Electronic limiters are invisible unless
        recorded.</strong> Many EVs and most German cars are limited well below
        the speed the physics allows. Where a limiter is known it is used; where
        it is not, the figure shown is what the car could do unrestricted, and
        it will exceed the published number — sometimes by a lot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">It has no torque curve.</strong> Acceleration
        assumes a fixed fraction of peak power is available throughout the run
        rather than modelling an engine sweeping its rev range and losing time
        to shifts. That fraction is the model's largest single approximation and
        it is listed in the constants above.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">Consumption is a steady cruise, not a drive
        cycle.</strong> It models holding a constant speed on level ground, so
        it is not comparable with a WLTP or EPA figure and must not be read as
        one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">Braking is the physical floor.</strong> It
        assumes every tyre is at peak grip for the whole stop, and ignores fade,
        weight transfer and whether the brakes could actually reach the tyres'
        limit — which for many older cars they could not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-ink">Air density is standard.</strong> No correction
        for altitude or temperature, both of which move real drag by several
        percent.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The Markey Score" lede={SCORE_METHODOLOGY_NOTE}>
    <dl class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-5 text-sm">
      {
        Object.entries(SCORE_WEIGHTS).map(([factor, weight]) => (
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 border-b border-line pb-2 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <dt class="capitalize text-ink-secondary">{factor.replace(/([A-Z])/g, ' $1')}</dt>
            <dd class="type-data font-medium tabular-nums">{(weight * 100).toFixed(0)}%</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="max-w-prose pb-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      A car is never scored on data it does not have. Missing sub-factors are
      excluded and the remaining weights renormalised, and where fewer than half
      the factors have data behind them the blended number is withheld entirely
      rather than shown with false confidence. Scoring a missing figure as zero
      would rank the least-documented cars last, which measures our research
      rather than the car.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    title="Depreciation"
    lede="A projection from a published formula. Not market data, and not a valuation."
  >
    <dl class="flex flex-col gap-3 py-5 text-sm">
      {
        Object.entries(DECAY_BY_POSITIONING).map(([tier, decay]) => (
          <div class="border-b border-line pb-3 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <dt class="flex justify-between gap-4">
              <span class="capitalize">{tier.replace('-', ' ')}</span>
              <span class="type-data font-medium tabular-nums">
                {(decay.rate * 100).toFixed(0)}% / yr
              </span>
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{decay.basis}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="max-w-prose pb-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Decay is exponential — a percentage of remaining value each year — because
      that is how cars actually behave. A linear model would reach zero on a
      specific date, which no car does. If you log real valuations against a car
      in your garage, they are plotted on the same chart as points, and the
      curve is deliberately <em>not</em> refitted to them: the divergence is the
      interesting part.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Insurance risk" lede={RISK_DISCLAIMER}>
    <dl class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-5 text-sm">
      {
        Object.entries(RISK_WEIGHTS).map(([factor, weight]) => (
          <div class="flex justify-between gap-4 border-b border-line pb-2 last:border-b-0 last:pb-0">
            <dt class="capitalize text-ink-secondary">{factor.replace(/([A-Z])/g, ' $1')}</dt>
            <dd class="type-data font-medium tabular-nums">{(weight * 100).toFixed(0)}%</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Still to be published here">
    <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 py-5 pl-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <li>
        The wind tunnel's validation numbers against published drag coefficients
        for canonical shapes — sphere, cube, flat plate and teardrop — so its
        accuracy can be judged rather than trusted.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
